{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>And if you bring.</b> Here אִם functions in the sense of כִּי, for this is not optional — Scripture is speaking of the meal-offering of the omer, which is an obligation; so too ״וְאִם יִהְיֶה הַיֹּבֵל״ etc. (Numbers 36)." "<b>A meal-offering of first fruits.</b> Scripture is speaking of the meal-offering of the omer, which comes in the spring — at the time the produce ripens, and it comes from barley. אָבִיב is said here and אָבִיב is said there (Exodus 9): הַשְּׂעֹרָה אָבִיב." "<b>Roasted in fire.</b> That they dry it over the fire in a roasting-pipe — for otherwise it cannot be ground in a mill, because it is moist." "<b>Gruel of Carmel.</b> Crushed while still moist." "<b>Gruel.</b> גֶּרֶשׂ is a term of breaking and grinding: crushed in a mill of coarse groats, as in ״וַיַּגְרֵס בֶּחָצָץ״ (Lamentations 3), and so too ״גָּרְסָה נַפְשִׁי״ (Psalms 119)." "<b>כרמל.</b> While the כַּר is still full — the produce still moist and full of its stalks; therefore the early ears are called כַּרְמֶל. So too ״כַּרְמֶל בְּצִקְלוֹנוֹ״ (II Kings 4)."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T09:04:56.797626505Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}